     **Verified:** backend — full suite (65/65, up from 64) passes, including a new regression test for the wall-clock flush fix; explicitly checked for leaked background tasks (`asyncio` "Task was destroyed but it is pending" warnings) across the suite and found none, since every direct `TickIngestBridge` construction (test and route-driven) now has a `stop()` path. Frontend — `tsc -b` and `vite build` both succeed, with the only remaining errors being the pre-existing, already-flagged `GridPresetPicker.tsx` dead code (decision #35); the SPA-fallback question above was checked against a real `vite preview` server (`curl` returning `200` for both `/` and `/window/mw-1`), not assumed. **Not verified:** no live browser click-through of any of the five items — same caveat decisions #40 and #41 already carry forward, now a third consecutive round of UI work landing on inspection/build-check verification alone. Worth treating as the next priority before further UI work stacks on top of it.
 
-No open items remain blocking Phase 1–3. Phase 3's live-data path is now fully built end to end for both real-time streaming (`FinnhubAdapter`, decision #32) and historical backfill (`PolygonAdapter`, decision #30 — though see #39's structural caveat: minute-level backfill specifically isn't available on Polygon's free tier either), coordinated through the two-role registry (decision #33) — both auto-connect on startup if configured, feeding the same `TickIngestBridge` → Event Bus → WebSocket Gateway pipeline Phase 2 proved out, and now actually reaching the chart (decision #35) rather than stopping at the backend. **Finnhub confirmed working live, twice, on two separate real sessions:** a genuine WebSocket handshake against `wss://ws.finnhub.io` with working keepalive ping/pong, and — in the second session — five real symbol subscriptions all succeeding (`TSLA`, `NVDA`, `AAPL`, `MSFT`, `AMD`, all `200 OK`). The earlier "No streaming provider connected" report was not seen again in that second session; the restart-race theorized as the likely cause (a file-triggered `--reload` restart landing in the same window as a subscribe request) was never independently confirmed, but nothing since has reproduced it either. `IBKRAdapter` remains unverified against a real live connection (account access still pending). Feature Engine work has now started on the frontend chart-reading side (decisions #40–41: SMA, EMA, VWAP, Previous Day Close/High/Low, Pre-Market High/Low, Camarilla Pivots, VPOC) — the backend Feature Engine itself (formal `Feature`/`FeaturesUpdated` publishing per `trading-intelligence-architecture.md`) is still not started, and five of the seven chart-reading indicators above (everything but SMA/EMA/VWAP) won't show real levels yet since they need multi-day history this app doesn't have until #39 resolves. Next natural checkpoint: click-through verification of decisions #40–42 in a real browser (three rounds deep without one now — see #42's own verification note), then continuing the backend Feature Engine — Participation and the two new Context providers (decisions #19–20) — and, separately, revisiting the Timescale question against real backtesting requirements in Phase 4/5. Manual trading, Execution Mode, the Input Layer, and the hotkey/action model (decisions #22–27) are gated on Phase 5/6 — Governor, Trade Planning Engine, and Execution Engine don't exist yet — and land alongside that work, not before it.
+43. **The `candles`/`symbols` tables (scaffolded Phase 2, unused since) now have a writer and a reader — self-recorded intraday history closes the gap decision #39 first flagged, without waiting on a paid data plan.** Reported symptom: with the market open and Finnhub streaming live, a sub-window showed candles only from the moment it connected forward — reconnect, refresh, or open a second sub-window on an already-tracked symbol, and there was nothing before the first live bar, even though this app had already seen and closed earlier candles for that exact symbol in the same run.
+
+    **Root cause wasn't a bug to patch — it was a documented, unbuilt piece of the architecture.** `system-design.md` §4.2 (Market Data Engine) has said "Persist candles/ticks to PostgreSQL via a write-behind recorder (non-blocking)" since it was written; Phase 2 built the `candles`/`symbols` schema and migration for exactly this, and the migration's own TODO said outright: "automate future-partition creation... before this becomes a real operational gap — i.e. before Market Data Engine is actually writing candles." Nothing had ever subscribed to `CandleClosed` to actually write to it. This entry is that piece — pulled forward from the full Phase 4 Market Data Engine (`ConnectionManager` → `Normalizer` → `StateCache` → `Publisher` + `HistoricalWriter` as independent async tasks) into the current Phase 3 pipeline, not a reimplementation of it: `CandleRecorder` (`app/services/candle_recorder.py`) is just the `HistoricalWriter` half, wired directly onto the existing `EventBus` rather than a formal internal queue architecture that doesn't exist yet.
+
+    **New pieces:**
+    - `CandleRecorder` subscribes to `CANDLE_CLOSED` and persists every closed 1m candle — TickIngestBridge's fixed bucket size is the only granularity ever recorded, matching the frontend's own "always fetch 1m, resample client-side into 5m/15m/1h/4h/1d" design (decision #35, `resample.ts`) — there's no reason to store anything coarser separately. Non-blocking by construction, not just in name: `EventBus._consume()` `await`s `asyncio.gather()` over every handler for an event before pulling the next one off its queue (`bus.py`), so a slow synchronous DB write done *inside* the subscriber callback would block live price fan-out for every other subscriber on the same lane — the opposite of "non-blocking." The subscriber callback here only pushes onto an in-memory `asyncio.Queue` (no I/O) and returns; a separate background task drains it and performs the actual write via `asyncio.to_thread` (the DB engine is synchronous by design per `app/db/session.py` — same `to_thread` pattern `PolygonAdapter` already uses for its own sync REST calls). `TickIngestBridge`'s own accepted-race note (decision #42) is reused here too: a genuine duplicate `CandleClosed` for the same minute is rare and already accepted, so writes use `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, not a competing `DO UPDATE`.
+    - `app/db/partitions.py` — `ensure_month_partition()`, called before every write, closes that exact migration TODO: `candles` is RANGE-partitioned with no catch-all partition (deliberately, for query-pruning), so an unrecognized month rejects every insert outright. This app is running past the two months the original migration seeded (July/August 2026) *today* — this was not a someday concern. Idempotent, self-healing, always ensures the current month and the next one, so a write landing right at a month boundary is never the first thing to discover the next partition doesn't exist.
+    - `app/services/candle_store.py` — `get_recorded_candles()`, the read side. `GET /market/candles` (`market.py`) now checks this first, before ever reaching an external provider: for `1m` specifically this is the *only* source that can ever exist at all on a free-tier Polygon/Massive plan (see #39, and this entry's own Massive-rebrand note below), so self-recorded data isn't a fallback, it's the primary path. An external provider is only reached when literally nothing has been recorded yet for a symbol (brand-new ticker, or a freshly-migrated/empty database) — a partial match (recorded history shorter than the requested range) is returned as-is rather than topped up from Polygon, since Polygon structurally can't fill a 1m gap anyway. A DB that's unreachable is treated as "nothing recorded yet" (caught, logged, falls through), not a hard failure — the whole path is an optional enhancement at this phase, same soft-fail posture main.py already applies to a missing Finnhub/Polygon key.
+    - `main.py` — `CandleRecorder` starts unconditionally in `lifespan()`, independent of whether Finnhub/Polygon end up connected, right after the Event Bus starts. Stops cleanly on shutdown, same `stop()` pattern decision #42 established for `TickIngestBridge`.
+
+    **A second, adjacent, pre-existing bug found and fixed by the same real-DB test run, not assumed:** `Candle.id` in `app/models/market_data.py` didn't declare `Identity()`, even though its own migration's DDL does (`sa.Identity()`) — the ORM model and the schema it's supposed to describe had disagreed since Phase 2, invisible until this was the first code that ever actually inserted a row and SQLAlchemy warned about a composite-PK column with no known default generator. Fixed to match.
+
+    **On the "why is Polygon behaving inconsistently" question this surfaced:** Polygon.io has rebranded to Massive.com (some time in 2026, after training-data cutoff — confirmed via their current knowledge base, not assumed from stale docs); same API and keys, new company name. Checked against that current source: the free ("Stocks Basic") plan is end-of-day/daily-only — no minute-level aggregates at all, consistent with (not contradicting) #39's `NOT_AUTHORIZED` finding. The "2 years of historical data" figure attached to the free tier in various secondhand sources refers to how far back that daily data goes, not that minute bars are included — so a `1d`-timeframe request behaving very differently from a `1m` one on this same free key is expected, not a bug. Separately, and not yet fixed (flagged, consciously deferred rather than silently left): `polygon_provider.py`'s `if not aggs: raise SymbolNotFoundError` conflates "provider says this symbol doesn't exist" with "this specific window had zero trades" (e.g. a weekend/holiday query) — TSLA is obviously a valid ticker, so an empty-but-authorized response for it was being misreported as an invalid one. Lower priority now that self-recorded data is the primary `1m` path and Polygon is only ever reached as a last-resort fallback, but still worth fixing before it confuses a real "is this symbol wrong" investigation.
+
+    **On whether running Finnhub + Polygon "at the same time" was itself the problem, as asked:** no — role separation (decision #33: Finnhub claims streaming when configured, Polygon always claims historical) isn't a coordination bug and isn't what this entry fixes. The actual gap was a *capability* gap in whichever provider held the historical role — Polygon's free tier structurally cannot serve `1m` data at any window, and Finnhub's own historical endpoint is separately paywalled (decision #32) — not a conflict between the two running concurrently. The fix wasn't "run them differently," it was "stop depending on either one for the specific data type neither can provide, and self-supply it from what's already flowing through the pipeline this app already has."
+
+    **Verified:** a real local PostgreSQL 16 instance, not mocked — installed, started, migrated (`alembic upgrade head` against the actual, unmodified `0001` migration), and used for every claim in this entry. `ensure_month_partition()` checked directly: called for a date in a month with no existing partition, confirmed the partition didn't exist beforehand and did exist after, via `pg_tables`. `CandleRecorder` + `candle_store` checked end-to-end through the real `EventBus` (publish `CandleClosed` → confirm the row lands → read it back and match every field) — `tests/test_candle_recorder.py`, including the duplicate-close-doesn't-double-write case. The route itself checked separately, through the actual FastAPI app via `TestClient`, with `broker_registry` deliberately empty (no provider connected at all) — `test_market_candles_serves_self_recorded_data_with_no_provider_connected`, `test_market_routes.py`. Graceful degrade checked by stopping Postgres entirely and re-running: the 3 DB-dependent tests skip (not fail — `pytest.mark.skipif`, not a bare assumption that CI/a fresh clone has Postgres running), the other 69 (65 pre-existing + this entry's 4 new) still pass unaffected, and a real `uvicorn` process with the DB down still boots and answers `GET /market/candles` with the expected "nothing recorded, no provider connected" 400 rather than crashing or 500ing — confirmed via an actual request against a real running server, traceback and all, logged rather than raised. **Not verified:** the live-browser, market-hours version of the original report — i.e. actually watching a sub-window show pre-existing history immediately on connect during a real trading session — since today's a Sunday with the market closed; this is a str_replace-and-real-DB-test-level verification, not a click-through one, same caveat #40–42 already carry.
